WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:05.306
[MUSIC]

00:00:05.306 --> 00:00:14.575
[APPLAUSE]
&gt;&gt; Stacia,

00:00:14.575 --> 00:00:17.268
we are so
excited to have you today as you can see.

00:00:17.268 --> 00:00:22.544
You've said that Microsoft's mission
is not to be cool to make others cool?

00:00:22.544 --> 00:00:27.026
But Bob Dylan is playing across campus
today and you sold out faster than him.

00:00:27.026 --> 00:00:28.818
So,
&gt;&gt; [LAUGH]

00:00:28.818 --> 00:00:29.980
&gt;&gt; We certainly think Microsoft is cool.

00:00:29.980 --> 00:00:35.701
&gt;&gt; [APPLAUSE]
&gt;&gt; Really though, we're so

00:00:35.701 --> 00:00:39.286
grateful to you for making the time to
be with us, and we have a lot to cover.

00:00:39.286 --> 00:00:42.031
But I thought we could start
with your childhood in India.

00:00:42.031 --> 00:00:46.066
You've said that your father
never met a test he didn't ace,

00:00:46.066 --> 00:00:49.338
while your mother was
the opposite of a tiger mom.

00:00:49.338 --> 00:00:50.628
I love that contrast, and

00:00:50.628 --> 00:00:53.868
I'm curious how that different
life philosophy's shaped you.

00:00:53.868 --> 00:00:57.158
&gt;&gt; First of all, thank you so
much, Tara, for having me here.

00:00:57.158 --> 00:01:02.232
It's great to be at the GSB and
Bob Dylan was my idol.

00:01:02.232 --> 00:01:06.657
&gt;&gt; [LAUGH]
&gt;&gt; So times has been changing,

00:01:06.657 --> 00:01:09.164
it's interesting.

00:01:09.164 --> 00:01:14.063
In fact,
my father passed away just last month and

00:01:14.063 --> 00:01:21.191
I'm here reflecting quite a bit on
what both my parents have meant to me.

00:01:21.191 --> 00:01:26.700
My father was a Marxist economist and
a civil servant.

00:01:26.700 --> 00:01:31.619
And he had a definitive
point of view on what,

00:01:31.619 --> 00:01:36.413
life was all about and
the struggle in life.

00:01:36.413 --> 00:01:40.978
And he's right, I mean the guy
always used to look at my marks,

00:01:40.978 --> 00:01:46.337
I mean my score cards and used to be
amazed that somebody could be done bad.

00:01:46.337 --> 00:01:51.424
But luckily enough I think in some
sense he gave me in spite of all

00:01:51.424 --> 00:01:57.282
that a lot of confidence because to
him he felt like look it's marathon,

00:01:57.282 --> 00:02:00.466
you will catch up this is not that hard.

00:02:00.466 --> 00:02:04.905
And my mother was exactly the opposite of
course the only question she would ever

00:02:04.905 --> 00:02:06.237
ask me is are you happy?

00:02:06.237 --> 00:02:08.325
And I would say what
the heck does that mean,

00:02:08.325 --> 00:02:11.832
when you are reading my score card I
don't know if I should be happy or not.

00:02:11.832 --> 00:02:16.295
But again the two of them I think
when I look back growing up

00:02:16.295 --> 00:02:20.576
in Hyderabad in India in the late 70s,
early 80s.

00:02:20.576 --> 00:02:22.727
Interestingly enough, there are three or

00:02:22.727 --> 00:02:26.145
four of us, who now suddenly become
CEOs from the same high school.

00:02:26.145 --> 00:02:29.877
At that time was off the grid place.

00:02:29.877 --> 00:02:35.208
I think it was that ability to think.

00:02:35.208 --> 00:02:37.549
That ability to pursue your own passions.

00:02:37.549 --> 00:02:45.924
And have enough confidence
as well as some humility.

00:02:45.924 --> 00:02:50.931
Sort of when I look back,
I have been perhaps the biggest drivers

00:02:50.931 --> 00:02:55.684
of what sort of turned out to
be a reasonable several things.

00:02:55.684 --> 00:02:56.632
&gt;&gt; Absolutely, yeah.

00:02:56.632 --> 00:02:58.831
It's really clear how much
your parents influenced you.

00:02:58.831 --> 00:03:01.699
And another foundational influence for
you was sports.

00:03:01.699 --> 00:03:04.238
Now, my fellow international
classmates and

00:03:04.238 --> 00:03:07.307
I were mystified daily by
American sports references.

00:03:07.307 --> 00:03:09.679
So we're very happy to talk about cricket.

00:03:09.679 --> 00:03:10.313
&gt;&gt; Yeah.

00:03:10.313 --> 00:03:14.622
&gt;&gt; You love cricket, and
you dreamt of playing professionally.

00:03:14.622 --> 00:03:16.351
&gt;&gt; [LAUGH]
&gt;&gt; What lessons did you take

00:03:16.351 --> 00:03:16.977
from the pitch.

00:03:16.977 --> 00:03:22.746
Well, I mean, [LAUGH] all of us who are
South Asian are obsessed with that sport.

00:03:22.746 --> 00:03:26.640
And in fact, that's right, I mean,

00:03:26.640 --> 00:03:31.507
that was what I was pretty
much all bound up in all

00:03:31.507 --> 00:03:35.675
through my high school, into college.

00:03:35.675 --> 00:03:39.999
And, when I look back,
I think all sport teaches you a lot.

00:03:39.999 --> 00:03:43.728
And especially at least I feel team sport,

00:03:43.728 --> 00:03:49.021
I think has a huge impact and
how you think about leadership.

00:03:49.021 --> 00:03:53.355
I'll never forget this one
particular incident that I think

00:03:53.355 --> 00:03:55.571
subsequently written about.

00:03:55.571 --> 00:03:58.515
There's this guy who
was my school captain,

00:03:58.515 --> 00:04:02.553
who went on to do pretty well in
the context of Indian cricket.

00:04:02.553 --> 00:04:07.462
I was bowling trash that day and
he took over from me,

00:04:07.462 --> 00:04:11.157
got a wicket, which is a break though.

00:04:11.157 --> 00:04:13.170
But then he gave me the ball back and

00:04:13.170 --> 00:04:17.698
then I went on to have perhaps the best
bowling spell I've ever had in my life.

00:04:17.698 --> 00:04:20.876
And I always reflected as
to why did he do that?

00:04:20.876 --> 00:04:26.628
And then, in fact, much later on in life,
I went back and ask to meet with him.

00:04:26.628 --> 00:04:32.301
And so at least the way I surmise it as
a leadership decision he made of saying,

00:04:32.301 --> 00:04:37.650
look, he had recognized the importance
of not breaking my confidence.

00:04:37.650 --> 00:04:41.728
And I said like well that's
a pretty enlightened decision for

00:04:41.728 --> 00:04:45.503
a high school leader,
captain of a cricket team to make.

00:04:45.503 --> 00:04:47.794
And a lot of leadership lessons is that,
right.

00:04:47.794 --> 00:04:51.458
Which is you gotta make hard calls on,
say, performance, but

00:04:51.458 --> 00:04:55.331
also you got be able to sort of
understand that you need your team and

00:04:55.331 --> 00:04:58.950
it's not like everybody is going to
have a good day all the time.

00:04:58.950 --> 00:05:03.132
And that saddled distinction and
that judgement, right,

00:05:03.132 --> 00:05:08.063
which is one of the things that I feel
which is the most understated part

00:05:08.063 --> 00:05:11.928
of leadership is judgement,
and it's so important.

00:05:11.928 --> 00:05:17.536
And that judgement comes by you exercising
this muscle around passing judgement and

00:05:17.536 --> 00:05:18.809
learning from it.

00:05:18.809 --> 00:05:20.521
And I thought that's one
of the lessons I learned.

00:05:20.521 --> 00:05:22.598
&gt;&gt; He was building a confidence in a way,
by making-

00:05:22.598 --> 00:05:23.570
&gt;&gt; That's right, that's right.

00:05:23.570 --> 00:05:24.653
&gt;&gt; So you didn't end up playing
professional cricket, oddly.

00:05:24.653 --> 00:05:28.346
[LAUGH]
&gt;&gt; Here I am.

00:05:28.346 --> 00:05:31.833
&gt;&gt; [LAUGH]
&gt;&gt; But you did make you way to the US and

00:05:31.833 --> 00:05:34.673
soon after you started your own family.

00:05:34.673 --> 00:05:38.422
And in reading your book, something that
really moved me was how much you talk

00:05:38.422 --> 00:05:42.358
about your family and the role they've
played bringing empathy into your life.

00:05:42.358 --> 00:05:45.785
Could you share with us how
being a parent has shaped you?

00:05:45.785 --> 00:05:50.641
&gt;&gt; Yeah, it's a very big part of what is

00:05:50.641 --> 00:05:54.728
perhaps shaped my world view.

00:05:54.728 --> 00:05:57.365
And for both my wife and
I grew up together.

00:05:57.365 --> 00:05:59.420
We went to the same schools.

00:05:59.420 --> 00:06:03.050
And we were the only
children of our parents.

00:06:03.050 --> 00:06:07.665
So when we were both in our late 20s,
when our first son was about to be born,

00:06:07.665 --> 00:06:09.017
we were very excited.

00:06:09.017 --> 00:06:10.722
The household was all about.

00:06:10.722 --> 00:06:15.099
My wife is an architect and
she was practicing at the time.

00:06:15.099 --> 00:06:19.396
And so my only concern was when
will I go back to school, or

00:06:19.396 --> 00:06:21.199
would I go back to work.

00:06:21.199 --> 00:06:25.815
And how are going to think about
the babies, a day care and what a view.

00:06:25.815 --> 00:06:31.498
And lo and behold one night
there was some complications and

00:06:31.498 --> 00:06:39.240
our son Zain was born because of some
complications, he now has cerebral palsy.

00:06:39.240 --> 00:06:44.012
He's got gas quadriplegic and
is locked in.

00:06:44.012 --> 00:06:50.636
And I would say for the first maybe as
many as five years, I struggled with it.

00:06:50.636 --> 00:06:55.786
Primarily, because I felt that
all these plans that I had for

00:06:55.786 --> 00:07:00.635
what our life was going to be like,
had taken a real turn.

00:07:00.635 --> 00:07:05.631
And I watch AnuGo up and
down five trading, Seattle.

00:07:05.631 --> 00:07:09.909
Taking him to every therapy possible,
speech, occupational.

00:07:09.909 --> 00:07:16.669
And then I was just watching it and still
moaning, my own sort of whatever issues.

00:07:16.669 --> 00:07:21.749
But then it dawned on me that
nothing actually happened to me.

00:07:21.749 --> 00:07:24.731
Something had happened to my son and

00:07:24.731 --> 00:07:28.814
that I needed to as a father step up and
do my duty.

00:07:28.814 --> 00:07:31.191
In other words, it is the harsh but

00:07:31.191 --> 00:07:36.780
real lesson around being able to see the
world through the eyes of my son, right?

00:07:36.780 --> 00:07:38.476
That's what empathy's all about.

00:07:38.476 --> 00:07:43.968
And I think that's what it is, I mean,
it's innate in us all as humans.

00:07:43.968 --> 00:07:49.584
I think empathy is something
that we're all very capable of,

00:07:49.584 --> 00:07:54.886
life teaches us that and
in small ways, and in tough ways.

00:07:54.886 --> 00:08:00.423
Like I remember even by the last
interview when I was interviewing

00:08:00.423 --> 00:08:04.977
at Microsoft was also My
life changing moment for me.

00:08:04.977 --> 00:08:06.866
So I went through this interview.

00:08:06.866 --> 00:08:09.239
It was all caught on screen at that time.

00:08:09.239 --> 00:08:13.840
And so, this guy sort of says hey,
here's a question for you.

00:08:13.840 --> 00:08:19.530
You're at the crossroads, a baby falls,
and is crying, what will you do?

00:08:19.530 --> 00:08:23.320
And I say, wow, this is some
search algorithm I didn't learn.

00:08:23.320 --> 00:08:27.650
It must be some variant of some
traveling salesman problem or something.

00:08:27.650 --> 00:08:29.690
And I sort of really thought about it for
a few minutes.

00:08:29.690 --> 00:08:33.360
And then I said I'll go to
the phone booth and call 911.

00:08:33.360 --> 00:08:34.860
This is pre-smartphone.

00:08:36.250 --> 00:08:38.630
And so, he gets up.

00:08:38.630 --> 00:08:44.463
He escorts me out, and he says you know
what, you need to develop some empathy.

00:08:44.463 --> 00:08:49.470
Because when a baby falls, you pick them
up and hug them first before you call.

00:08:50.690 --> 00:08:53.275
And I thought that's it,
I'm definitely not going to get this job.

00:08:53.275 --> 00:08:58.277
&gt;&gt; [LAUGH]
&gt;&gt; And lo and behold, I did get the job.

00:08:58.277 --> 00:09:01.706
But nevertheless, I mean,
I really think that that's so core.

00:09:01.706 --> 00:09:05.196
And some people say well, what what does
that have to do with sort of business?

00:09:05.196 --> 00:09:07.873
Or what does it have to do with work?

00:09:07.873 --> 00:09:10.260
And I believe it has
everything to do with work.

00:09:10.260 --> 00:09:14.840
I believe if you sort of say innovation
is all about meeting unmet unarticulated

00:09:14.840 --> 00:09:20.220
needs customers,
where is that source of your ability

00:09:20.220 --> 00:09:23.860
to get in touch with that unmet
unarticulated need going to come from?

00:09:23.860 --> 00:09:26.920
It's going to come from your
ability to in some sense

00:09:26.920 --> 00:09:32.110
be able to listen between the lines,
to be able to extrapolate.

00:09:32.110 --> 00:09:34.462
And that's, to me,
deep sense of empathy, right?

00:09:34.462 --> 00:09:36.410
So people talk about design thinking.

00:09:36.410 --> 00:09:38.350
I think design thinking is empathy.

00:09:38.350 --> 00:09:43.640
And so therefore, I do believe
that life teaches you empathy.

00:09:43.640 --> 00:09:48.565
Empathy is the source to success
in any innovative agenda you have.

00:09:48.565 --> 00:09:52.597
&gt;&gt; It's inspiring to hear you say
that you think empathy is innate.

00:09:52.597 --> 00:09:56.150
We're living in a world where sometimes
it feels like empathy is on the decline.

00:09:56.150 --> 00:09:59.431
So I think your point around
empathy is really well taken.

00:09:59.431 --> 00:10:05.210
&gt;&gt; It's a great question, because in fact,
it is such a big word, and it is hard.

00:10:05.210 --> 00:10:09.966
In fact, even recently at Microsoft,
we realized that it's important for us

00:10:09.966 --> 00:10:15.200
to even understand what are the necessary
conditions to even develop empathy.

00:10:15.200 --> 00:10:20.510
So one of the words we have put into
our sort of parlance is respect.

00:10:21.530 --> 00:10:24.760
For example,
if you don't start from a place so

00:10:24.760 --> 00:10:28.708
having some respect for the other
person's views, where they come from,

00:10:28.708 --> 00:10:32.616
their complete history,
it's really hard to develop empathy.

00:10:32.616 --> 00:10:36.034
So therefore, I think you're
absolutely right to say before

00:10:36.034 --> 00:10:38.862
you think about some of
these higher level things,

00:10:38.862 --> 00:10:43.152
you have to sort of even question what
are the basics that we need to get right.

00:10:43.152 --> 00:10:46.308
&gt;&gt; Certainly, and that's become so
core to your leadership at Microsoft.

00:10:46.308 --> 00:10:49.652
So if we turn now to Microsoft,
before you were CEO,

00:10:49.652 --> 00:10:53.992
you took this job leading Microsoft's
first real Cloud business.

00:10:53.992 --> 00:10:58.006
And Steve Ballmer apparently told you
this might be your last job at Microsoft

00:10:58.006 --> 00:11:00.967
because if you fail, there's no parachute.

00:11:00.967 --> 00:11:03.137
That feels like a lot of pressure.

00:11:03.137 --> 00:11:05.261
Why did you make that jump?

00:11:05.261 --> 00:11:10.179
&gt;&gt; [LAUGH] Steve,
who went to school here, had many-

00:11:10.179 --> 00:11:12.769
&gt;&gt; [LAUGH]

00:11:12.769 --> 00:11:13.967
&gt;&gt; He just went for one year,

00:11:13.967 --> 00:11:15.113
from what I understand.

00:11:15.113 --> 00:11:18.125
&gt;&gt; [LAUGH]
&gt;&gt; This is the funniest thing I should

00:11:18.125 --> 00:11:21.960
tell I guess the other guy who were
to school here was Mukesh Ambani.

00:11:21.960 --> 00:11:26.843
So I believe one day, Steve Ballmer
is introducing Mukesh in Bombay,

00:11:26.843 --> 00:11:31.228
and he joked that both of us dropped
out of GSB except that Mukesh

00:11:31.228 --> 00:11:34.980
had never told anybody that
he had dropped out of GSB.

00:11:34.980 --> 00:11:36.643
So there was a real scandal.

00:11:36.643 --> 00:11:41.507
&gt;&gt; [LAUGH]
&gt;&gt; So Steve wanted

00:11:41.507 --> 00:11:46.357
us to really take this new business area,

00:11:46.357 --> 00:11:50.480
which is our online business.

00:11:50.480 --> 00:11:55.610
In fact, Susan, who is here, and
I worked on it for a long time as well.

00:11:55.610 --> 00:11:59.240
And he felt that look, this is a place
where we needed to make progress.

00:11:59.240 --> 00:12:04.410
And Steve had this very clear
sense of what it means to succeed.

00:12:04.410 --> 00:12:07.140
And his point was you're
going to go learn a lot.

00:12:07.140 --> 00:12:12.903
And of course, I'll fire you if
you don't do a good job with it.

00:12:12.903 --> 00:12:18.393
And it is sort of a way for
him to perhaps communicate both why he,

00:12:18.393 --> 00:12:21.864
as the CEO, cared about this business.

00:12:21.864 --> 00:12:24.502
And at the same time, his expectations.

00:12:24.502 --> 00:12:30.268
But I must say, that particular tour
of duty has been the most influential

00:12:30.268 --> 00:12:36.871
in how I've sort of thought about whether
it's distributed computing at its core,

00:12:36.871 --> 00:12:41.997
whether it's the economic models
that are going to be emergent.

00:12:41.997 --> 00:12:43.394
And that has been very, very helpful.

00:12:43.394 --> 00:12:47.222
Doing different things inside the company
has helped me grow to run the company

00:12:47.222 --> 00:12:47.918
eventually.

00:12:47.918 --> 00:12:53.215
So I actually appreciate Steve
both giving me that opportunity,

00:12:53.215 --> 00:12:57.645
and more importantly giving
me the message that look,

00:12:57.645 --> 00:13:02.790
at the end of the day,
it's performance that matters.

00:13:02.790 --> 00:13:06.250
&gt;&gt; Did you know at the time that that
decision was putting you on a path to one

00:13:06.250 --> 00:13:07.090
day becoming the CEO?

00:13:07.090 --> 00:13:07.665
&gt;&gt; No way.

00:13:07.665 --> 00:13:09.980
&gt;&gt; [LAUGH]
&gt;&gt; Most people ask me this,

00:13:09.980 --> 00:13:14.340
which is like hey, did you have
a sense that you'll become CEO?

00:13:14.340 --> 00:13:16.909
No.

00:13:16.909 --> 00:13:20.985
First of all, none of us grew up even,
to us, especially having grown up at

00:13:20.985 --> 00:13:24.806
Microsoft, I don't think I even
thought of a Microsoft where Bill and

00:13:24.806 --> 00:13:26.850
Steve were not actively engaged.

00:13:26.850 --> 00:13:31.805
I mean, it was just not even
in the realm of possibility.

00:13:31.805 --> 00:13:36.314
The thing, though, I would say,
especially business school graduates that

00:13:36.314 --> 00:13:40.770
are an ambitious lot, and you're all sort
of saying when am I going to be my CEO?

00:13:40.770 --> 00:13:45.470
My only advice there would
be it's like don't wait for

00:13:45.470 --> 00:13:47.750
your next job to do your best work.

00:13:47.750 --> 00:13:49.955
That I think is the crux of it,

00:13:49.955 --> 00:13:55.430
which is if you think about every job
you get, as the most important job and

00:13:55.430 --> 00:14:00.130
as the thing that is perhaps your
last job, but you gave it all.

00:14:00.130 --> 00:14:03.183
And of course, from there,
a lot of things will happen.

00:14:03.183 --> 00:14:07.555
And that's at least how I, it was not like
the job that I had before becoming CEO I

00:14:07.555 --> 00:14:11.415
somehow thought well, it's just
a stop on the way to something else.

00:14:11.415 --> 00:14:16.160
I actually thought that was a fantastic
job until I got the next job.

00:14:16.160 --> 00:14:20.482
&gt;&gt; And that next job was ultimately
to become the CEO of Microsoft.

00:14:20.482 --> 00:14:24.367
And when you took that job,
you faced some high expectations.

00:14:24.367 --> 00:14:26.460
Microsoft is struggling.

00:14:26.460 --> 00:14:28.380
You're following,
as you mentioned, Bill Gates,

00:14:28.380 --> 00:14:33.310
Steve Ballmer, and many people thought
that the CEO should come from outside.

00:14:33.310 --> 00:14:34.986
So what did you draw on in that moment?

00:14:37.893 --> 00:14:41.025
&gt;&gt; Yeah, I mean, look,
I'm a consummate insider.

00:14:41.025 --> 00:14:43.745
I've grown up at Microsoft now, 28 years.

00:14:45.749 --> 00:14:49.900
And I also recognize, to your point,

00:14:49.900 --> 00:14:54.584
that I was taking over from Steve and
Bill.

00:14:54.584 --> 00:14:59.934
And Steve even though was not a founder,
he had founder status in the company.

00:14:59.934 --> 00:15:02.119
Bill and Steve built the company.

00:15:02.119 --> 00:15:04.124
Bill and Paul founded the company.

00:15:04.124 --> 00:15:09.106
And the one thing that perhaps I
was more grounded having worked

00:15:09.106 --> 00:15:14.276
with them is as a non-founder CEO,
I needed to make explicit some

00:15:14.276 --> 00:15:19.080
of the things that I think founder
CEOs can take for granted.

00:15:19.080 --> 00:15:23.432
Because founder CEOs can carry
just because of who they are and

00:15:23.432 --> 00:15:26.537
what they mean to the organization, a lot.

00:15:26.537 --> 00:15:29.603
And telegraph that very broadly and
have followership.

00:15:29.603 --> 00:15:32.028
Whereas I describe myself
as a mere mortal CEO.

00:15:32.028 --> 00:15:38.940
I felt I needed that sense of
purpose to be deeply rekindled.

00:15:38.940 --> 00:15:42.420
It's something that I re-enforced.

00:15:42.420 --> 00:15:43.324
And culture.

00:15:43.324 --> 00:15:45.930
Both sort of these two pillars
of sense of purpose and

00:15:45.930 --> 00:15:48.810
culture needed to become
much more explicit.

00:15:48.810 --> 00:15:51.760
For example,
when I joined Microsoft in '92,

00:15:51.760 --> 00:15:56.940
we used to talk about our mission as
putting a PC in every home and every desk.

00:15:56.940 --> 00:15:58.233
It was pretty inspiring, right?

00:15:58.233 --> 00:16:00.025
I mean,
you could even do an Excel spreadsheet.

00:16:00.025 --> 00:16:00.969
P times Q.

00:16:00.969 --> 00:16:03.807
It is easy to compute.

00:16:03.807 --> 00:16:07.770
And And except by the late 90s,
we had more or

00:16:07.770 --> 00:16:10.470
less achieved that at least
in the developed world.

00:16:10.470 --> 00:16:14.407
And since then,
we had the struggle of what's next.

00:16:14.407 --> 00:16:17.015
And I felt like I needed to
sort of go back, in fact,

00:16:17.015 --> 00:16:19.215
to the very origin of the company, right?

00:16:19.215 --> 00:16:23.015
I mean,
Microsoft got started when we built up,

00:16:23.015 --> 00:16:27.753
Paul and Bill, built the BASIC
interpreter for the Altair.

00:16:27.753 --> 00:16:32.303
And I believe that everything that
needs to be known about Microsoft in

00:16:32.303 --> 00:16:37.084
2019 can be traced back to our origin,
which is rebuild technology so

00:16:37.084 --> 00:16:39.920
that others can build more technology.

00:16:39.920 --> 00:16:42.900
Right, I felt like we were doing
things out of envy and others.

00:16:42.900 --> 00:16:47.290
We needed to get back to what
our core identity is, right?

00:16:47.290 --> 00:16:51.720
Especially in 2019 where every company
is a software company around the world.

00:16:51.720 --> 00:16:55.300
We can just basically be a software
platform and tools provider and

00:16:55.300 --> 00:16:56.570
have a good business.

00:16:56.570 --> 00:16:59.040
And so
I felt let's be proud of who we are.

00:16:59.040 --> 00:17:03.000
Of course,
we got to express it differently, and

00:17:03.000 --> 00:17:05.030
then really reinforce that.

00:17:05.030 --> 00:17:07.640
That's why we talk about our mission
around empowering people and

00:17:07.640 --> 00:17:09.490
organizations.

00:17:09.490 --> 00:17:13.997
The other piece, though,
is we had to work on our culture.

00:17:13.997 --> 00:17:18.993
I distinctly remember,
I guess it was 98 when we first

00:17:18.993 --> 00:17:23.685
became the largest market
cap company in the world.

00:17:23.685 --> 00:17:29.658
And many of us were lucky enough to
participate on that wave of growth.

00:17:29.658 --> 00:17:33.753
But I remember that day when you we
walked around, you could see in campus,

00:17:33.753 --> 00:17:36.877
people thought wow,
we must be God's gifts to mankind.

00:17:36.877 --> 00:17:40.610
&gt;&gt; [LAUGH]
&gt;&gt; Right, we're so smart, we're so good,

00:17:40.610 --> 00:17:43.740
look at us, and
except that was not the case.

00:17:43.740 --> 00:17:47.540
I mean,
the case was it's a temporal thing.

00:17:47.540 --> 00:17:52.540
And what matters is your ability to learn,
grow,

00:17:52.540 --> 00:17:56.590
be grounded in the realities and
customers and what have you.

00:17:56.590 --> 00:18:02.476
And so that's why I wanted a culture that
stood for that learning organization.

00:18:02.476 --> 00:18:07.639
And in fact, my wife had introduced
me to a book by a Stanford professor

00:18:07.639 --> 00:18:13.620
Carol Dweck, which I'd read Mindset
many years before I became CEO.

00:18:13.620 --> 00:18:16.220
It was a huge influence in our household

00:18:16.220 --> 00:18:19.260
as it relates to our own
children's education.

00:18:19.260 --> 00:18:21.420
But quite frankly,
it was a great education for me.

00:18:21.420 --> 00:18:24.740
Because when I read that
book I realized that notion

00:18:25.750 --> 00:18:28.290
of growth mindset applies
to individuals like me.

00:18:28.290 --> 00:18:30.230
It applies to companies like Microsoft.

00:18:30.230 --> 00:18:34.915
So we took that meme of growth mindset and
said, look, let's not be know-it-alls,

00:18:34.915 --> 00:18:36.531
let's be learn-it-alls.

00:18:36.531 --> 00:18:40.188
And it's been a very helpful
part of I would say our

00:18:40.188 --> 00:18:44.106
journey around this what is
a cultural meme that we can

00:18:44.106 --> 00:18:48.307
even make first class that we
can have a real dialogue on.

00:18:48.307 --> 00:18:51.858
&gt;&gt; Absolutely, and
you talk a lot about this cultural shift.

00:18:51.858 --> 00:18:54.489
In looking back at that
over the last five years,

00:18:54.489 --> 00:18:56.870
what was the most
difficult part to change?

00:18:56.870 --> 00:18:59.840
Because you instilled growth mindset,
you did all of these things.

00:18:59.840 --> 00:19:02.145
But what were the hardest roadblocks?

00:19:02.145 --> 00:19:04.930
&gt;&gt; I think it's always, it's a challenge.

00:19:04.930 --> 00:19:06.557
It's one thing to say growth mindset.

00:19:06.557 --> 00:19:09.705
Because sometimes people will come
to me and say that Microsoft, Satya,

00:19:09.705 --> 00:19:12.158
we found the ten people who
don't have a growth mindset.

00:19:12.158 --> 00:19:15.542
&gt;&gt; [LAUGH]
&gt;&gt; The point about that it's

00:19:15.542 --> 00:19:17.915
not about going looking for ten people.

00:19:17.915 --> 00:19:23.294
It's about me being comfortable with
confronting my own fixed mindset each day.

00:19:23.294 --> 00:19:24.860
And that's the hard part of it.

00:19:24.860 --> 00:19:27.691
When you say you're a learning
organization and you say, yep,

00:19:27.691 --> 00:19:28.798
we've learnt the that.

00:19:28.798 --> 00:19:31.206
Here are the ten people
who have not learnt.

00:19:31.206 --> 00:19:32.529
Then that's a problem.

00:19:32.529 --> 00:19:36.408
And I think that that's the reality of it,
right?

00:19:36.408 --> 00:19:38.214
How do you bring about long-term change?

00:19:38.214 --> 00:19:39.321
And I'll say one other thing.

00:19:39.321 --> 00:19:46.013
One of the reasons why I feel
at least we have some momentum.

00:19:46.013 --> 00:19:51.297
I'm very careful not to sort of paint
this as some destination we will reach or

00:19:51.297 --> 00:19:52.940
have reached for sure.

00:19:52.940 --> 00:19:56.240
It's going to be something that every
day we're going to have to confront our

00:19:56.240 --> 00:19:56.950
fixed mindset.

00:19:56.950 --> 00:19:59.600
We're always going to be imperfect.

00:19:59.600 --> 00:20:05.050
There is going to be a gap between
what is our espoused sort of culture

00:20:05.050 --> 00:20:07.650
and what is the lived experience.

00:20:07.650 --> 00:20:11.382
The question is are we
working to bridge that gap?

00:20:11.382 --> 00:20:16.099
And it's very uncomfortable especially
in business where everything is graded

00:20:16.099 --> 00:20:18.250
by how close to perfection are you?

00:20:18.250 --> 00:20:22.150
To say let's be imperfect and celebrate
imperfection, it's just a hard thing.

00:20:22.150 --> 00:20:25.168
And that's why leadership at the top,

00:20:25.168 --> 00:20:30.026
setting the tone, walking the walk,
I think is the hard part.

00:20:30.026 --> 00:20:32.260
But anyway, that's the the real,

00:20:32.260 --> 00:20:36.367
I would say challenge of being
able to implement change at scale.

00:20:36.367 --> 00:20:40.428
&gt;&gt; Absolutely, and you want to set this
culture of a growth mindset where people

00:20:40.428 --> 00:20:43.251
can take risks and
make mistakes and learn from them.

00:20:43.251 --> 00:20:47.159
And you want to walk the walk.

00:20:47.159 --> 00:20:50.070
When have you had to lead
by example on this front?

00:20:50.070 --> 00:20:54.400
&gt;&gt; Well, I mean,
every day I would say but in some sense,

00:20:54.400 --> 00:21:01.030
the decision one makes in, for me,

00:21:02.070 --> 00:21:07.154
the ability to sort of,
take even diversity and inclusion.

00:21:10.110 --> 00:21:16.020
Saying the words, saying we're
going to make progress is one thing.

00:21:16.020 --> 00:21:21.496
And then to recognize that progress
has to be something that really

00:21:21.496 --> 00:21:26.598
has to come from one's own first
change in behavior, right?

00:21:26.598 --> 00:21:32.950
And take the everyday experience of
the senior leadership team meeting itself.

00:21:33.960 --> 00:21:34.992
It's an interesting thing.

00:21:34.992 --> 00:21:39.650
Every time I question myself on
everything that we talk about,

00:21:39.650 --> 00:21:44.480
as what we espouse that is more
broadly applicable across the company.

00:21:44.480 --> 00:21:47.540
How much of it is represented
in the behaviors,

00:21:47.540 --> 00:21:51.360
starting with me of our own
senior leadership team?

00:21:51.360 --> 00:21:56.570
We have some very, very amazing women
who are part of our leadership team.

00:21:56.570 --> 00:22:01.170
Are they participating like anybody else?

00:22:01.170 --> 00:22:03.660
And in fact, am I allowing for

00:22:03.660 --> 00:22:07.750
them to be able to really make sure
that we are listening to them?

00:22:07.750 --> 00:22:11.270
They are able to feel like
they're driving the company.

00:22:11.270 --> 00:22:15.440
And starting with that type of
sensibility, which by the way,

00:22:15.440 --> 00:22:16.280
is not constant.

00:22:16.280 --> 00:22:17.568
But at least I'll be pushing.

00:22:17.568 --> 00:22:21.386
It's sort of the way I think I remind
myself of how important to your boy.

00:22:21.386 --> 00:22:25.660
But it's sort of putting
it in everyday practice.

00:22:25.660 --> 00:22:30.275
We have three things that are the cultural
I would say pillars for us, right,

00:22:30.275 --> 00:22:32.690
one is diversity and inclusion.

00:22:32.690 --> 00:22:34.990
The other one is customer obsession.

00:22:34.990 --> 00:22:39.250
And then the other one is to bring the
company together as one company as opposed

00:22:39.250 --> 00:22:42.220
to fragmented setup PnLs.

00:22:42.220 --> 00:22:46.356
All three of these are just super hard,
easy to say, but

00:22:46.356 --> 00:22:49.790
require everyday practice
starting with me.

00:22:49.790 --> 00:22:53.432
&gt;&gt; Certainly, and I think diversity and
inclusion is an interesting one because

00:22:53.432 --> 00:22:55.930
it's a topic that can invoke
a lot of defensiveness.

00:22:55.930 --> 00:23:00.268
So to apply a growth mindset to
that area is particularly powerful.

00:23:00.268 --> 00:23:05.970
&gt;&gt; Yeah, for example,
the thing that we have recognized is

00:23:05.970 --> 00:23:08.410
you have to put, I mean you have
to do a lot of things here.

00:23:08.410 --> 00:23:11.420
For example, we even changed
the compensation of our senior leaders

00:23:11.420 --> 00:23:16.830
starting, or even mine, to ensure
that we take this as a huge priority.

00:23:16.830 --> 00:23:21.547
You could say, well,
is the compensation change the real thing?

00:23:21.547 --> 00:23:23.947
I'm not saying it's the only
thing that needs to change, but

00:23:23.947 --> 00:23:25.020
it's an important thing.

00:23:25.020 --> 00:23:30.643
I myself had probably perhaps not
recognized that measuring things,

00:23:30.643 --> 00:23:33.501
having a real metric around it, and

00:23:33.501 --> 00:23:38.279
then compensating is a good sort
of start on a lot of fronts.

00:23:38.279 --> 00:23:43.186
But the thing that we've come to realize
is every intern class that joins

00:23:43.186 --> 00:23:47.323
Microsoft every year is more
diverse than the previous one.

00:23:47.323 --> 00:23:53.260
But then they look around and say, well,
where's that diversity in the company?

00:23:53.260 --> 00:23:59.987
And so that means, the real currency
of a culture is inclusiveness.

00:23:59.987 --> 00:24:06.590
And that, I think is The core
job of what leaders do,

00:24:06.590 --> 00:24:11.760
and what is the everyday experience
of what happens at Microsoft.

00:24:11.760 --> 00:24:17.810
Interestingly enough, I've also come
to recognize that first level manager,

00:24:18.860 --> 00:24:23.070
has probably the most
influence on what happens.

00:24:23.070 --> 00:24:28.180
And so, I distinctly remember as a lead
at Microsoft with five people working for

00:24:28.180 --> 00:24:32.040
me, who all were thinking,
why is this guy leading us, right?

00:24:32.040 --> 00:24:36.100
I mean, it's always the case, because the
first level job is the hardest job because

00:24:36.100 --> 00:24:40.190
there are five people who are also just
recent graduates who are sort of looking

00:24:40.190 --> 00:24:43.260
at you and saying, I can do what you do.

00:24:43.260 --> 00:24:46.530
And then you have a boss who's
asking you to do many things.

00:24:46.530 --> 00:24:51.230
And so it's sort of real pressure cooker,
in the sense you sort of really have to

00:24:51.230 --> 00:24:56.010
do a lot, but that's when your
attitude your nurturing of that

00:24:56.010 --> 00:25:00.450
five people who are working for you
around inclusiveness, will matter a lot.

00:25:00.450 --> 00:25:02.480
So one of the things that
we are doubling down is,

00:25:02.480 --> 00:25:08.000
are we truly supporting
managers at all levels,

00:25:08.000 --> 00:25:12.860
to be able to sort of in fact
support their team so that they can

00:25:12.860 --> 00:25:17.750
bring the best out of them, and then have
them feel included as part of the company.

00:25:18.900 --> 00:25:20.460
&gt;&gt; So we've talked a lot about culture.

00:25:20.460 --> 00:25:24.560
Let's shift and talk a bit about strategy,
and particularly the Cloud.

00:25:24.560 --> 00:25:26.790
Because today,
we sort of take the Cloud for granted.

00:25:26.790 --> 00:25:29.680
But when you took over,
it was still far from a short thing.

00:25:29.680 --> 00:25:34.760
And you made this bold bet,
cementing Microsoft strategy in the Cloud.

00:25:34.760 --> 00:25:36.680
How did you rally all
those around that vision,

00:25:36.680 --> 00:25:39.610
when many were convinced it wouldn't work?

00:25:39.610 --> 00:25:44.276
&gt;&gt; I mean, our challenge with
the Cloud was very, very,

00:25:44.276 --> 00:25:49.660
very successful business in what
was the client server error.

00:25:50.800 --> 00:25:55.060
And sort of you look at ADP&amp;L that says,
here's a new business.

00:25:55.060 --> 00:25:58.640
By the way, it's got gross
margins that are sort of 1/4

00:25:58.640 --> 00:26:02.390
of this gross margins of the current
business, and it will be good.

00:26:02.390 --> 00:26:07.830
It's hard to sort of look at
those kind of transitions,

00:26:07.830 --> 00:26:14.240
because all rationality says that you
should avoid it as much as possible.

00:26:14.240 --> 00:26:18.280
And except in tech in particular,in
many other businesses,

00:26:18.280 --> 00:26:21.070
these transitions are secular,
they're inevitable.

00:26:21.070 --> 00:26:26.690
And so the question is,
how do you make that transition?

00:26:26.690 --> 00:26:27.670
And that's where I must say,

00:26:27.670 --> 00:26:31.815
Steve, when he was a CEO was
the one who gave me permission.

00:26:31.815 --> 00:26:37.210
And the decision he made was,
look, we want to go after this.

00:26:37.210 --> 00:26:39.735
And one of the things that
I've realized as leaders, and

00:26:39.735 --> 00:26:43.350
many of you in your career are going to
make this kinds of decisions.

00:26:43.350 --> 00:26:47.595
We're all about solving this
over constrain problem, right?

00:26:47.595 --> 00:26:51.535
I mean, classic over constrain problem
as we have a huge profit margin.

00:26:51.535 --> 00:26:57.820
And now you gotta go build a new business,
that sort of replaces this business.

00:26:57.820 --> 00:27:00.860
And by the way, you should have
the same gross profit margin.

00:27:00.860 --> 00:27:02.300
And guess what?

00:27:02.300 --> 00:27:03.860
It's not possible.

00:27:03.860 --> 00:27:08.310
So someone somewhere has
to remove some constraint.

00:27:08.310 --> 00:27:08.850
Who does it?

00:27:08.850 --> 00:27:09.880
Leaders do it.

00:27:09.880 --> 00:27:13.140
So you remove the constraint
,called gross margin.

00:27:13.140 --> 00:27:15.100
He said go in this market.

00:27:15.100 --> 00:27:18.290
And that's what was then made it possible,
for

00:27:18.290 --> 00:27:23.400
us to do all those things that have
now gotten us to the other side.

00:27:23.400 --> 00:27:25.080
But to me, I've learned a lot from that.

00:27:25.080 --> 00:27:28.950
So in fact, if anything,
I feel like as a leader, sometimes you

00:27:28.950 --> 00:27:33.400
get to speak from both sides of the mouth,
which is I need growth and I need profit.

00:27:33.400 --> 00:27:38.860
In many cases, your job as leaders is to
in fact unconstrained, take on the risk.

00:27:38.860 --> 00:27:44.040
In fact in the first multiple years,
Amy Hood who's my CFO and

00:27:44.040 --> 00:27:47.140
myself we said look,
let us take on the risk.

00:27:47.140 --> 00:27:51.000
And then actually metric
a lot of our leaders,

00:27:51.000 --> 00:27:58.190
more on customer satisfaction
usage versus profit, revenue even.

00:27:58.190 --> 00:28:02.940
And I think that that's sort of
the type of decision making one needs,

00:28:02.940 --> 00:28:05.150
in order to make these harsh transitions.

00:28:05.150 --> 00:28:08.790
&gt;&gt; And when you look back and you look at
all you've done on culture, and strategy,

00:28:08.790 --> 00:28:09.680
and making this ball bud,

00:28:09.680 --> 00:28:13.970
are you able to tell which one moved
the needle more for Microsoft's renew.

00:28:16.230 --> 00:28:20.775
&gt;&gt; I fundamentally believe
that strategies or

00:28:20.775 --> 00:28:28.282
markets will always be coming and
going, there will be lots of changes.

00:28:28.282 --> 00:28:33.197
I'm a fundamental believer
in that sense of purpose and

00:28:33.197 --> 00:28:38.113
culture as the two pillars
that are necessary in order to

00:28:38.113 --> 00:28:40.952
get a lot of other things right.

00:28:40.952 --> 00:28:44.249
Of course,
if you don't get your strategy right, or

00:28:44.249 --> 00:28:48.610
your ability to sort of right
a particular wave of innovation.

00:28:48.610 --> 00:28:52.480
And especially in tech,
it's pretty harsh, right?

00:28:52.480 --> 00:28:55.650
I mean, it's very hard to recover.

00:28:55.650 --> 00:28:59.330
But that said though, the question is
what is it that will give you the best

00:28:59.330 --> 00:29:02.120
probability of even catching those.

00:29:02.120 --> 00:29:06.336
And to me that sends a purpose,
which I think is reflection of what you're

00:29:06.336 --> 00:29:08.992
innately good at as an organization,
right?

00:29:08.992 --> 00:29:14.590
It's competitive advantage,
except it's codified in that identity.

00:29:14.590 --> 00:29:19.791
And culture is what allows you to express
that identity with new opportunity.

00:29:22.044 --> 00:29:26.664
&gt;&gt; I think many of us in this room want to
use our careers to create impact at scale,

00:29:26.664 --> 00:29:29.954
and you have committed almost
30 years to Microsoft,

00:29:29.954 --> 00:29:33.280
which seems unfathomable to many of us.

00:29:33.280 --> 00:29:35.595
How do you think about-
&gt;&gt; Flies by.

00:29:35.595 --> 00:29:39.260
[LAUGH]
&gt;&gt; How do you think about

00:29:39.260 --> 00:29:44.220
entrepreneurship, and this idea for those
among us who maybe want to work in large

00:29:44.220 --> 00:29:47.850
organizations to create change in
the world, what advice would you have?

00:29:47.850 --> 00:29:52.770
&gt;&gt; Yeah, I mean look,
I mean I think that all organizations,

00:29:52.770 --> 00:29:56.350
small or large,
all have amazing opportunities.

00:29:56.350 --> 00:30:00.400
But I'll make the case for
a large organization,

00:30:00.400 --> 00:30:02.230
and especially an organization
like Microsoft.

00:30:02.230 --> 00:30:05.220
I mean think about it,
if you want to have an impact at scale,

00:30:05.220 --> 00:30:09.150
in fact one of the reasons,
there are two things.

00:30:09.150 --> 00:30:12.310
When I'm going to recruit at a college,
I'm always telling people, like hey look,

00:30:12.310 --> 00:30:14.200
if you want to be cool,
go join somebody else, but

00:30:14.200 --> 00:30:16.330
if you want to make others
cool join Microsoft.

00:30:16.330 --> 00:30:20.050
And I say that because I believe that

00:30:20.050 --> 00:30:24.860
which other organization will be
able to have in 190 plus countries,

00:30:24.860 --> 00:30:29.100
the impact on small business productivity,
public sector efficiency,

00:30:29.100 --> 00:30:33.370
multinationals in any part of the world
and their competitiveness globally.

00:30:33.370 --> 00:30:34.982
Health outcomes, education outcomes.

00:30:34.982 --> 00:30:38.733
This is scale at enormous rate.

00:30:38.733 --> 00:30:43.183
And so therefore if you want,
join a company like this, but

00:30:43.183 --> 00:30:48.620
then you have to have the following,
I'd say sensibility.

00:30:48.620 --> 00:30:50.135
Guess what, you've gotta work with others.

00:30:50.135 --> 00:30:53.760
[LAUGH] In other words,
that's I think a key important skill.

00:30:53.760 --> 00:30:54.550
Like, what does it mean?

00:30:54.550 --> 00:30:57.790
I mean, you do a lot of that, many of you
have got a lot of work experience even

00:30:57.790 --> 00:30:59.760
before coming to business school.

00:30:59.760 --> 00:31:03.310
But fundamentally,
to do anything useful, and big,

00:31:03.310 --> 00:31:06.140
and upscale, it's all about teams.

00:31:06.140 --> 00:31:08.910
Teams inside or teams outside.

00:31:08.910 --> 00:31:10.550
That's what it takes.

00:31:10.550 --> 00:31:15.480
And the second thing that you also need,
is to realize

00:31:15.480 --> 00:31:20.600
that a lot of people talk about the matrix
and the complexity of large organizations.

00:31:20.600 --> 00:31:21.810
That's again the case.

00:31:21.810 --> 00:31:25.030
I mean even for small companies,
you just have a different type of matrix.

00:31:25.030 --> 00:31:25.920
You'll have a VC.

00:31:25.920 --> 00:31:26.770
You'll have a board.

00:31:26.770 --> 00:31:28.430
You'll have a customers.

00:31:28.430 --> 00:31:32.430
So it's you're never going to
escape working with people.

00:31:32.430 --> 00:31:37.780
You're never going to escape bringing
multiple constituents together.

00:31:37.780 --> 00:31:42.740
And I think that you should pick whether
whatever size of organization recognize

00:31:42.740 --> 00:31:48.006
that scale only comes, because you bring
able to bring many constituents together.

00:31:48.006 --> 00:31:51.020
&gt;&gt; You say that we can never
escape working with people,

00:31:51.020 --> 00:31:53.795
which brings me to my next section on AI.

00:31:53.795 --> 00:32:00.290
&gt;&gt; [LAUGH]
&gt;&gt; You are a big component of AI, and you.

00:32:00.290 --> 00:32:04.800
Believe that AI will be good for humankind
and you argue that in an AI future,

00:32:04.800 --> 00:32:09.160
human traits like empathy and creativity
will be more important than ever.

00:32:09.160 --> 00:32:12.600
And yet in some ways,
technology has made us less connected.

00:32:12.600 --> 00:32:18.490
So how do you see AI augmenting humanity
rather than detracting from it?

00:32:18.490 --> 00:32:21.200
&gt;&gt; Look, first of all I'm excited
I'm going to spend sometime

00:32:21.200 --> 00:32:23.250
with your own work around this.

00:32:23.250 --> 00:32:28.651
Human centered AI and the work you're
doing there, I think it's so important.

00:32:28.651 --> 00:32:30.360
Here is how I come at it.

00:32:31.900 --> 00:32:36.770
First, before we get into some of
the unintended consequences of AI.

00:32:36.770 --> 00:32:42.540
One of the areas that I am deeply
involved in, is accessibility, right?

00:32:42.540 --> 00:32:47.250
Think about what AI has done to
people who need the most help, right?

00:32:47.250 --> 00:32:53.900
Say if you have,ALS now with Eyegaze,
you can type and communicate.

00:32:53.900 --> 00:32:59.520
If you have visual
impairment of any sorts,

00:32:59.520 --> 00:33:03.690
you can interpret the world by using
the latest in computer vision.

00:33:03.690 --> 00:33:06.940
If you have dyslexia be using
some machine reading and

00:33:06.940 --> 00:33:12.790
comprehension techniques you can start
teaching a middle school kid how to read,

00:33:12.790 --> 00:33:16.430
because reading then leads to their
participation in our economy.

00:33:16.430 --> 00:33:21.187
So I would first say, so AI and
say AI capabilities are helping more of us

00:33:21.187 --> 00:33:25.480
participate fully in our societies and
in our economies.

00:33:25.480 --> 00:33:29.306
That doesn't mean we shouldn't be
clear-eyed about the consequences of

00:33:29.306 --> 00:33:29.980
AI, right?

00:33:29.980 --> 00:33:33.820
If the first one is what
are the ethics of AI.

00:33:33.820 --> 00:33:37.340
We as in fact,
creators of these platforms and

00:33:37.340 --> 00:33:41.620
tools have to even before we even get
to sort of the big topic of ethics,

00:33:41.620 --> 00:33:46.280
improve the state of the art of
the software engineering around AI.

00:33:46.280 --> 00:33:51.680
So that things like bias and
so on are being dealt with.

00:33:51.680 --> 00:33:57.220
There's this fantastic line I love of
Real Case, which is he talks about how

00:33:57.220 --> 00:34:03.920
the future enters in you and transforms
itself in you before it becomes real.

00:34:03.920 --> 00:34:08.860
So we are creating AI, it's humans who
are creating AI, so we get to shape,

00:34:10.830 --> 00:34:14.710
what the craft of creation
of AI looks like,

00:34:14.710 --> 00:34:20.710
how we design systems where the human is
in we get to decide that as a society.

00:34:20.710 --> 00:34:23.030
What we are comfortable with and
what we are not comfortable with.

00:34:23.030 --> 00:34:27.400
So I don't want to
abdicate all of that and

00:34:27.400 --> 00:34:31.240
feel like this is just going to
happen outside of our control.

00:34:31.240 --> 00:34:35.610
So that's at least how I think about
it and also in the employment side,

00:34:36.640 --> 00:34:41.240
I do believe that for example, this.

00:34:41.240 --> 00:34:43.320
Is it zero sum?

00:34:43.320 --> 00:34:45.400
I don't think that is the case.

00:34:45.400 --> 00:34:46.940
I think that there will be more jobs.

00:34:46.940 --> 00:34:53.530
The question is, how do we really use, in
fact, all of the sort of levers we have,

00:34:53.530 --> 00:34:58.210
economic and social, to skill people for
the jobs that are going to be there?

00:34:58.210 --> 00:35:00.930
And many of those skills might be
different types of skills than

00:35:00.930 --> 00:35:03.070
the ones that are valued today.

00:35:03.070 --> 00:35:06.750
For example, there's no reason why our
society can't have wage support for

00:35:06.750 --> 00:35:07.940
teachers.

00:35:07.940 --> 00:35:12.930
And in a different way, in a world
where there's a lot of abundance of

00:35:12.930 --> 00:35:16.920
AI doing a lot of other things for
us, or artists, or what have you.

00:35:16.920 --> 00:35:19.250
So therefore,
I think we will come up with mechanisms.

00:35:20.480 --> 00:35:25.320
And I hope that we have, and
in fact, more than hope.

00:35:25.320 --> 00:35:30.270
I feel that we shouldn't abdicate our
responsibility to control our own

00:35:30.270 --> 00:35:32.540
future that I think we
all want to live in.

00:35:33.690 --> 00:35:36.750
&gt;&gt; And given Microsoft's
vision to democratize AI,

00:35:36.750 --> 00:35:41.270
there are concerns about what might happen
if those tools get into the wrong hands.

00:35:41.270 --> 00:35:45.170
And you've just mentioned not to
abdicate that sort of responsibilities.

00:35:45.170 --> 00:35:48.970
So how do you think about who to partner
with in light of those concerns?

00:35:48.970 --> 00:35:50.300
&gt;&gt; Right, there's multiple things.

00:35:50.300 --> 00:35:56.460
So, one is what is it that we
ourselves will do to, in some sense,

00:35:56.460 --> 00:36:02.660
have some core principles that define
what we do and then who we work with.

00:36:03.660 --> 00:36:06.390
Take facial recognition
that's quickly becoming

00:36:06.390 --> 00:36:09.830
the topic which I think is going to even
have regulatory frameworks around it.

00:36:09.830 --> 00:36:10.980
In the State of Washington,

00:36:10.980 --> 00:36:15.030
we participated even in
creation of that regulation.

00:36:15.030 --> 00:36:20.210
So I think that first before even
the regulation is in place, we have

00:36:20.210 --> 00:36:24.160
guidelines on what we think is the right
use because the maturity of the models

00:36:24.160 --> 00:36:30.010
really make it only sensible to use it
in certain use cases and not in others.

00:36:30.010 --> 00:36:35.920
And being clear about it and then ensuring
that that's what we do, and then working

00:36:35.920 --> 00:36:39.690
knowing that there will be like there's
food safety there should be AI safety.

00:36:39.690 --> 00:36:44.540
There will be regulation and
we should be okay with it.

00:36:44.540 --> 00:36:48.880
In fact even if anything our practices,
in our data of what's good, what's not.

00:36:48.880 --> 00:36:52.660
What's the state of technology
should inform that regulation.

00:36:52.660 --> 00:36:54.340
&gt;&gt; So
you bring up the topic of regulation.

00:36:54.340 --> 00:36:58.220
And today it seems that
relationships between regulators and

00:36:58.220 --> 00:37:01.350
many large tech companies are fraught.

00:37:01.350 --> 00:37:04.180
In its early days, Microsoft may
have fought its own battles but

00:37:04.180 --> 00:37:06.665
today Microsoft is leading with openness.

00:37:06.665 --> 00:37:11.283
And so what's your advice for
aspiring tech leader here on balancing

00:37:11.283 --> 00:37:16.155
that pressure to grow as a company
with your responsibility to society?

00:37:16.155 --> 00:37:19.121
&gt;&gt; That's an interesting question.

00:37:19.121 --> 00:37:23.480
Here's what I have at least learned.

00:37:23.480 --> 00:37:26.730
In fact, Brad Smith who is another
colleague of mine who recently wrote

00:37:26.730 --> 00:37:29.370
a book called Tools And Weapons.

00:37:29.370 --> 00:37:32.350
He reflects because he was very much part,
he worked for Bill.

00:37:32.350 --> 00:37:37.150
He worked with Steve and now he works with
me, very much part of our own struggle.

00:37:38.260 --> 00:37:43.570
I would say the one thing that we,
at least I took away from that time is,

00:37:43.570 --> 00:37:49.590
when there is, I'd say a lot of criticism
of whatever it is that you're doing,

00:37:49.590 --> 00:37:53.610
I think that it's appropriate for
us to look in the mirror and

00:37:53.610 --> 00:38:00.200
perhaps learn about what it is
that we're being criticized about.

00:38:00.200 --> 00:38:03.635
And perhaps there are changes that
we need to actually bring about.

00:38:03.635 --> 00:38:08.235
So I would say scrutiny of large
organizations is absolutely something that

00:38:08.235 --> 00:38:13.695
is going to only happen, and I think
large organizations should welcome it.

00:38:13.695 --> 00:38:17.984
And we all can learn from it.

00:38:17.984 --> 00:38:24.040
And the core though,
especially in technology business, we

00:38:24.040 --> 00:38:29.120
collectively as an industry have to just
mature and mature at a very fast rate.

00:38:29.120 --> 00:38:34.600
Because the impact of digital technology
in our lives and in our society is so

00:38:34.600 --> 00:38:39.820
deep now, that for us to assume that
we're just going to have unfettered

00:38:39.820 --> 00:38:45.180
access to all parts without

00:38:45.180 --> 00:38:50.370
thinking about the unintended consequences
of this technology are long gone.

00:38:50.370 --> 00:38:55.005
And so therefore, for
us as an industry, for us as a company,

00:38:55.005 --> 00:38:58.975
to both recognize the opportunity
of these technologies and

00:38:58.975 --> 00:39:02.465
the responsibilities, whether it's in
security, whether it's in privacy,

00:39:02.465 --> 00:39:05.980
whether it's in AI and ethics,
I think is going to be central.

00:39:05.980 --> 00:39:09.850
And these are not going to be
terms of competition, even.

00:39:09.850 --> 00:39:15.070
I think this is where the industriate
at scale, has to get a lot better.

00:39:15.070 --> 00:39:20.770
And I think that that moment is upon us,
and I do see positive change.

00:39:20.770 --> 00:39:26.940
But it is definitely a time for
self-reflection and change.

00:39:28.170 --> 00:39:32.730
&gt;&gt; So I want to circle back to your
own leadership style and values.

00:39:32.730 --> 00:39:37.680
And what strikes me is that you seem
to combine this sense of self and

00:39:37.680 --> 00:39:39.570
ability to stay true to
your leadership style.

00:39:39.570 --> 00:39:44.550
You even have a story of Steve Ballmer
telling you it's too late to be different.

00:39:44.550 --> 00:39:49.070
With, on the other hand,
this real belief in a growth mindset.

00:39:49.070 --> 00:39:49.610
And I think for

00:39:49.610 --> 00:39:52.870
many of us that the GSB there's this
question of how do you stay true

00:39:52.870 --> 00:39:57.960
to your own style while still knowing when
and how to grow in the right direction.

00:39:57.960 --> 00:40:01.450
And so
how do you think about those two things?

00:40:01.450 --> 00:40:04.159
Yeah, I mean,

00:40:06.537 --> 00:40:12.119
I think the ability,

00:40:14.498 --> 00:40:18.395
To understand yourself is actually,

00:40:18.395 --> 00:40:22.780
it's a lifetime's journey, right?

00:40:22.780 --> 00:40:26.963
It's a journey of your lifetime
to really unpack who you are,

00:40:26.963 --> 00:40:31.801
and what you are good at, what makes
you tick, what are your passions.

00:40:31.801 --> 00:40:35.125
It's something that, what makes you happy,

00:40:35.125 --> 00:40:39.473
the question that my mother asked
me all the time and in fact,

00:40:39.473 --> 00:40:45.570
the older I get, the clearer I am in
understanding even what she was asking.

00:40:45.570 --> 00:40:48.881
And then to be able to understand others,

00:40:48.881 --> 00:40:53.148
that's also another journey of a lifetime,
right?

00:40:53.148 --> 00:40:57.390
Which is you sort of feel like you
understand what others are saying.

00:40:57.390 --> 00:41:00.364
You understand where they're coming from.

00:41:00.364 --> 00:41:05.850
I think that's really what is
going to perhaps help you.

00:41:05.850 --> 00:41:10.939
With both be true to yourself,
your identity, what makes you tick,

00:41:10.939 --> 00:41:15.941
what makes you happy, while knowing
that ultimately I think a lot of

00:41:15.941 --> 00:41:20.975
that satisfaction you get is because
of your ability to empathize.

00:41:20.975 --> 00:41:25.250
The ability, it could be your family,
it could be your work place.

00:41:25.250 --> 00:41:26.858
That's the other thing that I have come

00:41:26.858 --> 00:41:28.767
to realize is we can be
transactional at work.

00:41:28.767 --> 00:41:33.727
I'll tell you there was this gentleman,
another GSB grad whom I work for,

00:41:33.727 --> 00:41:38.760
Doug Bergam, who i s actually
the governor of North Dakota nowadays.

00:41:38.760 --> 00:41:41.947
But there was this time in my mid 30s,

00:41:41.947 --> 00:41:46.596
he said something to me which
just had a profound impact.

00:41:46.596 --> 00:41:51.545
He said, look, you're going to
work at Microsoft more time

00:41:51.545 --> 00:41:56.099
than you are going to even
going to spend with your kids.

00:41:56.099 --> 00:42:00.310
And I said wow, that sounds pretty harsh.

00:42:00.310 --> 00:42:03.980
[LAUGH] And yet it is true.

00:42:03.980 --> 00:42:08.403
And his main point was you better
think about work having deeper meaning

00:42:08.403 --> 00:42:10.180
than being transactional.

00:42:11.960 --> 00:42:16.772
And as I've thought about that, the only
way it's not going to be transactional is

00:42:16.772 --> 00:42:19.197
when you relate to people you work with.

00:42:19.197 --> 00:42:22.800
And that's what you'll remember,
the projects you worked on.

00:42:22.800 --> 00:42:26.161
The technologies will
all be passe in time but

00:42:26.161 --> 00:42:30.406
the people what you did,
how you behave, what was your,

00:42:30.406 --> 00:42:36.437
I take great pride in these people whom
I've mentored or go on to do great things.

00:42:36.437 --> 00:42:40.769
That's the relationship that I think
you seek out a while being true to

00:42:40.769 --> 00:42:42.980
yourself and what makes you happy.

00:42:44.390 --> 00:42:47.547
&gt;&gt; And you mentioned humility earlier and
how important it is to you.

00:42:47.547 --> 00:42:49.461
And you have this quote that says,

00:42:49.461 --> 00:42:53.830
when everyone is celebrating you
is when you should be most sacred.

00:42:53.830 --> 00:42:57.731
And I think it's safe to say that, people
are celebrating Microsoft's renewal.

00:42:57.731 --> 00:43:02.000
So how do you keep yourself and
your teams grounded among the success?

00:43:02.000 --> 00:43:04.207
&gt;&gt; That's a great question.

00:43:04.207 --> 00:43:11.330
There's this book I recently read by
David Brooks called The Second Mountain.

00:43:11.330 --> 00:43:15.374
It was an interesting book because
he talks about it mostly in

00:43:15.374 --> 00:43:16.653
the context of us.

00:43:16.653 --> 00:43:21.226
There's that first mountain which
is what perhaps many of you as

00:43:21.226 --> 00:43:26.305
graduates of GSB are going to be on and
you're going to seek excellence and

00:43:26.305 --> 00:43:29.620
success in the professional career.

00:43:29.620 --> 00:43:35.060
And then you're eventually going to get
to that second mountain and then you're

00:43:35.060 --> 00:43:40.750
going to sort of, in his words, relate to
the world and community and what have you.

00:43:40.750 --> 00:43:44.140
And I feel that that's really
what's there for Microsoft.

00:43:44.140 --> 00:43:50.377
The way having gotten to
whatever high market cap in 98,

00:43:50.377 --> 00:43:54.833
now for
us it's more not our market cap but

00:43:54.833 --> 00:43:58.547
what is our market cap leading to?

00:43:58.547 --> 00:44:03.554
To your point, if you celebrate
our success but fundamentally

00:44:03.554 --> 00:44:09.610
realize if you sort of go back to our
mission and our business model, right?

00:44:09.610 --> 00:44:11.821
It's not even a sense of mission and

00:44:11.821 --> 00:44:17.142
purpose that somehow abstracted away from
what's core driving our business model.

00:44:17.142 --> 00:44:21.440
We need to see success all around us,
that's it.

00:44:21.440 --> 00:44:26.256
And so, if we celebrate that
small business in Kenya or

00:44:26.256 --> 00:44:29.609
that large multinational in Sweden or

00:44:29.609 --> 00:44:34.338
that public sector company in Indonesia or
Vietnam.

00:44:34.338 --> 00:44:41.720
That's what's going to help us
be grounded and be successful.

00:44:41.720 --> 00:44:46.482
And so that invoking of that everyday
sense of purpose is what I think is

00:44:46.482 --> 00:44:48.270
going to help us the most.

00:44:48.270 --> 00:44:49.584
&gt;&gt; Thank you Satya and

00:44:49.584 --> 00:44:54.880
I think on that note we will turn it over
to some questions from the audience.

00:44:54.880 --> 00:44:55.388
&gt;&gt; Okay.

00:44:55.388 --> 00:44:56.950
&gt;&gt; Is this thing on?

00:44:56.950 --> 00:45:01.651
Hi, my name is Tara Carrad Pear and
I am a first year MBA student.

00:45:01.651 --> 00:45:05.215
And I'm asking this question in conjuction
with my classmate Jeff Krueger who

00:45:05.215 --> 00:45:08.620
unfortunately couldn't be here but
we're both interested.

00:45:08.620 --> 00:45:12.808
You've mentioned that at Microsoft you've
made a principal decision that you're not

00:45:12.808 --> 00:45:16.317
going to withhold technologies from
institutions that have been elected

00:45:16.317 --> 00:45:18.713
in democracies to protect
the freedoms we enjoy.

00:45:18.713 --> 00:45:22.743
This position, as you probably know stands
in contrast to a number of your peer tech

00:45:22.743 --> 00:45:24.740
companies here in the valley.

00:45:24.740 --> 00:45:28.121
Can you elaborate on your
decision framework as CEO to have

00:45:28.121 --> 00:45:31.226
Microsoft Pursue US Department
of Defense contracts

00:45:31.226 --> 00:45:33.298
like the Jedi Cloud Project at DOD?

00:45:33.298 --> 00:45:38.287
&gt;&gt; Yeah, I mean, first of all,
one of the things that

00:45:38.287 --> 00:45:42.350
we're engaged in is with deep respect for

00:45:42.350 --> 00:45:50.480
all opinions that people may have on
things that we should be concerned about.

00:45:50.480 --> 00:45:55.408
But on this one, we've been very clear
from day one on the statement you read

00:45:55.408 --> 00:46:00.182
out which is I feel that I have great
belief in democratic institutions and

00:46:00.182 --> 00:46:01.878
our democratic process.

00:46:01.878 --> 00:46:07.774
And one of my big fears is that,
somehow CEOs or corporations try and

00:46:07.774 --> 00:46:14.083
substitute for what I think in the long
run is the most important thing for

00:46:14.083 --> 00:46:19.100
us, which is our democracy
to work as designed.

00:46:19.100 --> 00:46:23.425
And in this case,
if we don't like what our government does,

00:46:23.425 --> 00:46:28.764
we have this one great opportunity which
is we get to change, we get to vote.

00:46:28.764 --> 00:46:33.676
We get to even take principle stands
against our government if we do think

00:46:33.676 --> 00:46:36.910
that that is something
that we want to fight for.

00:46:36.910 --> 00:46:40.553
In fact, Microsoft, whether it's
in the previous administration or

00:46:40.553 --> 00:46:42.872
in this administration, we've had cases.

00:46:42.872 --> 00:46:49.610
It was the Warren case around privacy is
something that we were able to go fight.

00:46:49.610 --> 00:46:54.110
And then ultimately,
through the Cloud Act bring about change.

00:46:54.110 --> 00:47:00.274
Which was a bipartisan legislation, which
I feel is a good step in the direction

00:47:00.274 --> 00:47:06.240
of having privacy enshrined in
a legislative sort of set of processes.

00:47:06.240 --> 00:47:07.414
So that's how I look at it.

00:47:07.414 --> 00:47:12.641
I don't see how withholding technology
from I said the institutions

00:47:12.641 --> 00:47:16.965
that we have elected that
are subject to civilian control

00:47:16.965 --> 00:47:21.605
ultimately to protect the freedom
we enjoy is going to help.

00:47:21.605 --> 00:47:24.413
That doesn't mean we shouldn't
have ethical principles,

00:47:24.413 --> 00:47:26.732
we shouldn't advocate for
ethical principles.

00:47:26.732 --> 00:47:30.126
And in fact these institutions
that we're talking about have

00:47:30.126 --> 00:47:33.860
perhaps more of a history around
these ethical principles as well.

00:47:33.860 --> 00:47:36.935
And so for us to rely on that and
reinforce that I think would be important.

00:47:36.935 --> 00:47:40.256
&gt;&gt; Hi am Kashaan,

00:47:40.256 --> 00:47:44.603
